Database-level tests, run by `npm run test:db` via psql.

These exist because RLS and the double-booking constraint can only be
meaningfully tested inside the database. A policy that reads correctly in
review and a policy that actually blocks a cross-tenant SELECT are two
different things, and the difference is a patient-data breach.

Phase 1 adds:
  0001_rls_tenant_isolation.sql   Tenant A's JWT reads zero of Tenant B's rows
  0002_rls_anon_exposure.sql      anon reads zero rows from customers,
                                  bookings, notification_logs, business_members
  0003_rls_public_pii.sql         Public staff reads expose no phone or email
                                  (regression for defect B-05)
  0004_rls_write_scope.sql        Non-members cannot UPDATE businesses

Phase 2 adds:
  0010_double_booking.sql         50 concurrent inserts for one slot -> 1 wins
  0011_buffer_enforcement.sql     Adjacent bookings respect service buffers
                                  (regression for defect B-03)

Phase 7 adds:
  0020_role_matrix.sql            5 roles x ~20 actions

Convention: each file raises an exception on failure so psql exits non-zero.

  DO $$ BEGIN
    IF (SELECT count(*) FROM bookings) <> 0 THEN
      RAISE EXCEPTION 'tenant isolation broken: saw % rows', (SELECT count(*) FROM bookings);
    END IF;
  END $$;
